;
PLAYING GAMES

A - Playing games

:A
PLAYING GAMES

One of the more pleasant ways of spending time on an RCP/M is to play the
software instead of merely collecting it. The supplied GAMES volume contains
three prepared launchers:

    MOUNT GAMES
    J0:ADVENT
    J0:ANIMALS
    J0:MCHASE

Each launcher is a separate alternative to enter at the CP/M prompt after
mounting GAMES.

Adventure is the expanded version of the classic text adventure. Answer its
instructions prompt and follow the game's own commands.

Animals is the guessing game which asks questions to identify the animal
you're thinking of.

Maze Chase requires an ANSI/VT100-compatible display. If the screen is a mess
of stray characters, check your terminal emulation.

The launchers prepare the files the games need in WORK before starting the
actual game from the protected library disk. The supplied programs can
therefore write temporary data without modifying the shared software
collection.

Use a game's own exit command to return to CP/M. Temporary game data,
including any saved games and high scores in WORK, disappears when you log
off. You may be able to resume a game within the same call, but don't expect
its data to be there next week.

Some sysops install the optional software collections, including an expanded
GAMES volume. Read their catalogs and instructions. Not all archived games are
suitable for playing online; many are there for download and local use.
